# Integration, connectivity and app settings

An integration connects Home Assistant to a service or device. An app is software managed by an installation that supports apps. A discovered device, an integration entry, an app and a dashboard card are different objects. Keep their names and outcomes separate throughout setup and maintenance.

This reference pattern covers the management surfaces registered by frontend **20261028.5**, used by Core **2026.11.1**. It provides design ownership for the routes in the [interface coverage register](interface-coverage.html). It does not replace native integration forms, supply credentials or install software.

<h2 id="integration-capabilities">Use the actual management contract</h2>

| Surface | Show | Preserve |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Discovery and integration setup | Available identity, source and what setup would connect | The integration's native steps, supported options, permission checks and cancellation |
| Credentials and reauthentication | Which integration needs attention and the reported authentication problem | Native credential entry, secret masking, validation and authorised recovery |
| Connectivity and protocol panels | The selected adapter or network, devices, observations and permitted operations | The specific protocol's units, identifiers, pairing/reset consequences and support limits |
| Apps and marketplace | Installed versus available software, version, source and reported status | Installation-type restrictions, native update/install confirmation and permissions |
| Cloud and remote connection | Which connection is being configured and its reported state | Account scope, native authentication and an explicit explanation of remote access |

The pinned settings router includes Bluetooth, DHCP, infrared, LoRaWAN, Matter, Modbus, MQTT, radio frequency, serial, SSDP, Thread, Zeroconf, ZHA and Z-Wave JS entry points. Their presence in source does not mean they are enabled in every installation. KNX and Insteon links can open integration-owned panels. Apply the shared layout and state rules while retaining each panel's actual controls. Do not treat a protocol dashboard as evidence that every device exposes the same capabilities.

<h2 id="integration-discovery">Discovery is a setup opportunity</h2>

Use a clear name and any source-provided identifier that helps distinguish devices. Do not label a discovery as trusted, paired or ready before the relevant check succeeds. An ignored discovery, a removed integration entry and a deleted device have different effects. Explain the selected operation at its own confirmation step.

<%= example('admin-discovery') %>

<h2 id="integration-credentials">Keep credentials within their native flow</h2>

Show which connection needs reauthentication without displaying a token in an error message, example, screenshot or copied diagnostic. Preserve the user's place and any non-secret draft when the native flow supports it. If the backend outcome is not known, say that the result has not been confirmed. Do not translate a temporary timeout into a rejected password or grant a local visual control the authority to approve an account.

<%= example('admin-credential-status') %>

<h2 id="integration-protocols">Make protocol observations interpretable</h2>

Separate a device's last observation, an adapter's connection and an application's overall availability. Name the protocol-specific measure and unit. A count of discovered devices is not a count of working devices. Missing observations remain unknown; a signal-strength value alone is not a general health score. Preserve native pairing, inclusion, interview, configuration and reset wording when it matters to the task.

<%= example('admin-protocol-diagnostics') %>

<h2 id="integration-apps">Keep app lifecycle and automation state distinct</h2>

Use the current native **Apps** label when directing people to the interface, with “formerly add-ons” where it helps recognise older instructions. An app being installed does not establish that it is running; a running process does not establish that its connected devices work. Keep the reported state separate from a start, stop or update request. “Start on boot” is a setting, not an instruction to start immediately.

<%= example('admin-app-state') %>

<h2 id="integration-presentation">Shared appearance and accessible interaction</h2>

Use the [card](../components/cards.html), [summary list](../components/summary-list.html), [form](../components/controls.html), [error](../content/errors-and-alerts.html) and [dialog](../components/dialogs.html) contracts. Use quiet structural borders and the shared body scale. Preserve functional protocol icons with text names; do not replace them with unexplained decorative marks. Expose the selected network and action target in text, not just colour.

Maintain keyboard access through nested configuration flows, restore focus on cancellation and keep validation beside its field. A long hardware identifier must wrap visually without changing its copied value. Loading, permission denied, no results and disconnected are separate states. Do not replace a native protocol diagram with a table unless the alternative preserves the task; provide an accessible data or list view alongside it where possible.

<h2 id="integration-acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

1. Test supported and unsupported installation types, absent integrations, restricted accounts and a connection disappearing during setup.
2. Check discovery, configure, reauthenticate, disable, remove and delete separately. Confirm that each label describes the actual backend operation and target.
3. Test authentication failure, cancellation, timeout and a late response without exposing a secret or reporting unconfirmed success.
4. Verify protocol-specific identifiers, numeric units, selection and confirmations. Test keyboard navigation, 320px reflow, enlarged text and forced colours.
5. Verify app installed/running/updating/stopped states against actual responses. Keep version choice, update request and completion distinct.

The examples use invented records and local feedback. Native installation and device acceptance needs evidence for the actual Home Assistant version, integration and account. Follow [action results](action-results.html), [permissions](accounts-and-permissions.html), [connection recovery](connection-and-recovery.html) and the [compatibility register](compatibility-register.html).

